:pnlgrp.
.************************************************************************
.*  Help for command SNDPIPE
.************************************************************************
:help name='SNDPIPE'.
Send Record to Unix Cmd - Help
:p.The Send Record to Unix Cmd (SNDPIPE) command is always used
in conjunction with the Open Pipe (OPNPIPE) Command.  OPNPIPE
starts a QShell or PASE process in the background, and connects it
to your CL program using an interprocess communication object known
as a pipe.
.*
:p.The SNDPIPE command lets you send data from your CL program
through that pipe to the Unix process.
:p.:hp2.Restrictions::ehp2.
:ul.
:li.
You must previously open a pipe with the OPNPIPE command
before you run this.
.*
:li.
See the help for the OPNPIPE command for information about
the authorities needed to use this suite of tools.
:eul.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter RCD
.*******************************************
:help name='SNDPIPE/RCD'.
Record to send (RCD) - Help
:xh3.Record to send (RCD)
:p.Specifies one line of text data to be sent to the Unix process
that's receiving data from the connected pipe. You can run the
SNDPIPE command multiple times to send more data. Each call to
SNDPIPE will appear as a separate line of text to the Unix process.
:p.This is a required parameter.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.
Up to 5000 characters of data to be sent to the Unix process
over the pipe.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.*******************************************
.*   Help for parameter PIPEID
.*******************************************
:help name='SNDPIPE/PIPEID'.
Pipe ID to send to (PIPEID) - Help
:xh3.Pipe ID to send to (PIPEID)
:p.Tells the SNDPIPE command which pipe to send data to.  This is
particularly useful if you have more than one pipe open
simultaneously.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*DFT:epk.
:pd.
The default pipe is used. The default pipe is the one opened most
recently by the OPNPIPE command.
.*
:pt.:pv.decimal-number:epv.
:pd.
Specify the Pipe ID of an open pipe to send data to.
.*
:eparml.
:ehelp.
.**************************************************
.*
.* Examples for SNDPIPE
.*
.**************************************************
:help name='SNDPIPE/COMMAND/EXAMPLES'.
Examples for SNDPIPE - Help
:xh3.Examples for SNDPIPE
:p.:hp2.Example 1: Simple Command Example:ehp2.
:xmp.
SNDPIPE RCD('A horse is a horse, of course, of course')
:exmp.
:p.This command sends a line of text through a pipe to a
QShell or PASE process running in the background.
.*
:p.:hp2.Example 2: More Complex Command Example:ehp2.
:xmp.
OPNPIPE CMD('cat - > /tmp/examplefile.txt')
SNDPIPE RCD('This is some silly text to put in the IFS')
CHGVAR  VAR(&DATA) VALUE('And this is line 2.')
SNDPIPE RCD(&DATA)
CLOPIPE
:exmp.
:p.This command runs the 'cat' program in the QShell environment,
and requests that 'cat' write data to an IFS file named
/tmp/examplefile.txt.
:p.Two lines of text are written to the pipe and received by
the 'cat' program (which will, in turn, write them to disk.)
:p.Then, the pipe is closed with the CLOPIPE command.
.*
:p.:hp2.Example 3: Sending and Receiving Data
from a PHP script:ehp2.
:xmp.
OPNPIPE CMD('PATH=$PATH:/usr/local/Zend/Core/bin && +
             iconv -f 37 -t 819 | +
             php /www/zendcore/htdocs/geocode.php')
SNDPIPE RCD('1600 W Pennsylvania Av, Washington DC')
RCVPIPE RCD(&LAT)
RCVPIPE RCD(&LON)
CLOPIPE
SNDPGMMSG MSGID(CPF9897) MSGF(QCPFMSG) MSGTYPE(*DIAG) +
          MSGDTA('Lat=' *CAT &LAT *BCAT 'Lon=' *CAT &LON)
:exmp.
:p.This example opens a pipe to a PHP script named geocode.php.
The source code for geocode.php is available here:
http://systeminetwork.com/article.cfm?id=58488
:p.The SNDPIPE is used to write an address to the pipe.  The
pipe then invokes the QShell iconv command to convert the text
from CCSID 37 (EBCDIC) to CCSID 819 (ASCII). (You may not need
this, it depends on your environment.)  The output from iconv
is sent to the PHP script, which reads the address.
:p.Then, the PHP script uses the address to do some processing
(in this example, it calls a GeoCoding web service) and then
the PHP script writes the latitude and longitude of the address
back to the output pipe.
:p.The RCVPIPE command is used to receive the latitude and
longitude into the CL program.  The SNDPGMMSG command is used
to display that latitude and longitude.
:ehelp.
.**************************************************
